From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:10:06 -0400
Subject: round: a split keeps its folder, a closed folder in order, one chip
 name, no restore bar in full screen
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The round of 2026-10-14 on the operator's word, as one patch: its fixups
would conflict with the patches that changed the same lines since.

A split keeps its folder (folders F34, splits J8; BACKLOG S-136):
Chromium gathers the tabs to split by strip index, and a folder's first
and last slots resolve in its parent, so two tabs of a folder were split
outside it and a folder they emptied went. They gather into the pivot
tab's folder now, as into its tab group, and a split with a new tab keeps
the tab it splits as the pivot.

A closed folder comes back in its order (folders F19; BACKLOG S-135):
⇧⌘T judges the order by the window the tabs go back to, not the window
the command came from; where that window has fewer tabs than the
folder's place, every tab is added at its end, since a tab put back at
the folder's place once the window had grown past it landed in front of
the others or between a split's panes. The list records a closing split
before any tab of the close, so the close marks each tab's place in the
folder and the list is read in that order. The folder's first tab is
shown.

The chip under a drag (spaces B12; BACKLOG S-135): lit once the drag has
said what it carries, never at the lookup of the drop target; a chip
rebuilt during the drag keeps its bounds until the row is laid out, so a
drop on it lands.

A Space's name once (sidebar Y14; BACKLOG S-70): no tooltip on a chip in
the open sidebar, where the floating name shows; the rail keeps the
tooltip; the chip's accessible name is the Space's. The Mac's chord
badges on the chips go by each chip's Space.

The restore bar out of full screen (toolbar T33; BACKLOG S-69):
"Continue where you left off" asks to hide in full screen.

Why: the operator asked for S-135, S-136 and the other small defects
known, tested thoroughly; each change has its behaviour id and a test
that failed before the fix.
Removable when: never; these are Stedding's own behaviours.
